  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "The specimen fits the scenario at the boundary level: it keeps semantics in the pCID-selected payload, places CAS/body references inside payload protocols, and treats unknown payloads as opaque. That resists envelope backdoors, but it does not define CAS-backed group-session resolution or a cross-envelope equivalence rule, so independence is plausible rather than proven.",
    "strengths": [
      "Minimal [pcid, payload] envelope keeps application semantics out of the base wrapper.",
      "Content-addressed body references are explicitly left to payload protocols, which supports envelope independence.",
      "Unknown-pCID handling preserves exact bytes and fails explicitly instead of guessing."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The sim does not specify group-session semantics, so the scenario is only partially answered.",
      "Wrapper-selected signature/authorship protocols can still make meaning depend on nesting and authenticated byte boundaries.",
      "Opaque payloads reduce generic auditability unless the relevant handler specs remain available."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Different signature-wrapper pCIDs may authenticate different byte boundaries, weakening semantic equivalence across envelope variants.",
      "Long-term independence depends on durable pCID/spec preservation and migration evidence."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What invariant proves that two different envelope variants carrying the same resolved CAS payload represent the same group-session event?",
      "How should wrapper signatures be normalized so session semantics do not depend on outer-envelope bytes or nesting?",
      "What local evidence can Alice or Carol record to audit that equivalence without hidden global state?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
